<!--Render Target Documentation — Imports
  FQN: com.machineblocks.bml.target.render.documentation.Imports-->
<rdoc:Imports xmlns="http://blockml.org/bml"
  xmlns:core="org.blockml.bml.core"
  xmlns:rdoc="com.machineblocks.bml.target.render.documentation">

  <baseType>
    core:Documentation
  </baseType>
  <is>
    use and include paths emitted into each SCAD file
  </is>
  <documentation><![CDATA[
    Every generated geometry file imports the MachineBlocks V3 library and the
    selected ConfigSet. Paths are relative to the output file. The library path
    uses mbLibPath from targetOptions under the key machineblocks-v3-scad. The
    compiler RenderSession has no mbLibPath field. When mbLibPath is absent this
    slice returns files: [] and diagnostic BML_MB_LIB_PATH_MISSING.

    A ConfigSet file exports functions only. It imports neither the library nor
    another ConfigSet.

    Standard imports for a leaf, a composite, and a set (angle brackets are
    SCAD include syntax):

        use <{relative_path_to_scad_lib}/lib/block.scad>;
        use <{relative_path_to_the_ConfigSet_manifestation}>;

    The ConfigSet path is the output path under srcDir when the set lives in
    the project, using the same package mapping as the compile. A core set is
    mbLibPath/blocks/ plus the automatic manifestation, for example
    blocks/com/machineblocks/scad/config/DefaultConfigSet.scad.

    The customizer dropdown is built from that set's profiles, in profile
    order. The default is the bml:id of the first profile. Each option is
    bml:id:name. Hidden assigns hlp_config once from that set's function:

        /* [Config] */
        configProfile = "fine"; // [fine:Fine, standard:Standard]
        /* [Hidden] */
        hlp_config = mb__…__func__config(configProfile);

    A different set writes its own ids, labels, and function name. A profile
    without name returns files: [] and diagnostic MB_CONFIG_PROFILE_INCOMPLETE.

    Ordinary composite compositions render leaf instances as mb_block()
    (NativeBlock). They do not use-import dependency block SCAD files.

    Sets (com.machineblocks.bml.core.Set) reference typed block modules by the
    placed part's type FQN. The set processor emits a use import for each
    distinct type file, for example use <../wall/Wall.scad>, and each instance
    module calls that type's manifestation module.

    When expressions reference a #built-in call that is not a native SCAD
    mapping, the processor adds a use import for that service file, and only
    when the service is actually used:

        use <{relative}/blocks/org/blockml/bml/service/Math.scad>;
        use <{relative}/blocks/org/blockml/bml/service/Strings.scad>;

    Service SCAD files live in @machineblocks/core at
    blocks/org/blockml/bml/service/ (Math.scad, Strings.scad, Numbers.scad,
    Lists.scad, Bools.scad). They are pre-rendered artifacts. BML service
    definitions in @blockml/core are the SSOT. Import paths are always relative
    to the output file directory.
  ]]></documentation>
  <references>
    <see type="rdoc:NativeMappings" relation="ReadNext" />
  </references>
</rdoc:Imports>
